<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Bilevel optimization (BO) is widely applicable to many machine learning problems.
Scaling BO, however, requires repeatedly computing hypergradients, which involves solving inverse Hessian-vector products (IHVPs). In practice, these operations are often approximated using crude surrogates such as one-step gradient unrolling or identity/short Neumann expansions, which discard curvature information.
We build on implicit function theorem-based algorithms and propose to incorporate Kronecker-factored approximate curvature (KFAC), yielding curvature-aware hypergradients with a better performance efficiency trade-off than Conjugate Gradient (CG) or Neumann methods and consistently outperforming unrolling. We evaluate this approach across diverse tasks, including meta-learning and AI safety problems.
On models up to BERT, we show that curvature information is valuable at scale, and KFAC can provide it with only modest memory and runtime overhead. Our implementation is available at <a href="https://github.com/liaodisen/NeuralBo" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/liaodisen/NeuralBo</a>.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Bilevel optimization (BO), originally studied in economics <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">von Stackelberg, 1934</a>)</cite>, models interactions between two decision-makers: a leader who acts first and a follower who optimizes in response. This leader-follower structure captures scenarios where one optimization problem depends on another. In machine learning, BO provides a unifying framework for diverse tasks: in meta-learning to acquire transferable inductive biases <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bertinetto et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rajeswaran et al., 2019</a>)</cite>, in hyper-parameter and data optimization to adapt learning pipelines <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Franceschi et al., 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shu et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Feng et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lorraine et al., 2020</a>)</cite>, and in neural architecture search to guide model design <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2019</a>)</cite>. BO
also plays a central role in AI safety, covering adversarial
problems such as data poisoning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lu et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Radiya-Dixit et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2020</a>)</cite> and unlearnable examples <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. BO’s appeal
lies in its breadth: a single framework to reason about dependencies across learning, optimization, and safety.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Broadly speaking, algorithms for BO fall into three families: <em id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">gradient unrolling</em> (GU), <em id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">implicit function theorem</em> (IFT), and <em id="S1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">value-function</em> (VF). GU methods directly differentiate through a sequence of lower-level optimization steps <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Franceschi et al., 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shaban et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shen et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. While conceptually simple, they demand substantial memory and computation, and short unrolling horizons introduce truncation bias. IFT methods avoid explicit unrolling by leveraging the implicit function theorem to characterize the sensitivity of the lower-level solution <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lorraine et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Grazzi et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ji et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Choe et al., 2023b</a>)</cite>. These approaches circumvent the need to store the intermediate iterates and are therefore more memory-efficient than unrolling, but hinge on computing inverse Hessian-vector products (IHVPs), which becomes a major computational bottleneck in high dimensions. VF methods instead sidestep second-order computations by reformulating BO as a constrained single-level problem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kwon et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Giovannelli et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Despite avoiding explicit second-order operations, their practical scalability remains less clear: solving the resulting single-level formulation often introduces additional optimization variables and constraints, which can increase complexity and make them less competitive in large-scale stochastic learning settings <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Among the three, IFT-based approaches have recently gained popularity due to their ability to capture accurate hypergradients <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ghadimi and Wang, 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ji et al., 2021</a>)</cite>. Several efforts have pushed them toward larger-scale applications <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lorraine et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Choe et al., 2023a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Choe et al., 2023b</a>)</cite>. Since exact inversion of the Hessian is infeasible for modern models, practitioners often resort to crude approximations. A common strategy truncates the Neumann series after a few terms <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lorraine et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Choe et al., 2023a</a>)</cite>. However, full convergence is usually computationally out of reach at this problem size, and the neural net’s inner problem is frequently ill-conditioned <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sagun et al., 2016</a>)</cite>, making short truncations inaccurate. Others go further by replacing the Hessian with the identity matrix <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Choe et al., 2023b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hong et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2019</a>)</cite>, effectively collapsing IFT into a one-step unrolling method and discarding curvature information altogether. As a result, existing methods either degrade under poor conditioning or rely on overly simplistic surrogates
that produce inaccurate hypergradients.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Our work builds
squarely within the IFT territory, but seeks to address its central bottleneck: scalable IHVPs. To this end, we explore Kronecker-Factored Approximate Curvature <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">Martens and Grosse, 2015</a>, KFAC,)</cite>, which provides
an efficient block-diagonal curvature approximation using the (uncentered) covariance of layer inputs and gradients.
Although rarely used in BO, recent large-scale results on influence functions in language models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Grosse et al., 2023</a>)</cite> suggests that KFAC offers a practical middle ground between crude approximations and iterative curvature-aware methods. In contrast to influence functions, each outer step of IFT-based BO needs an IHVP, each of which is often obtained by iterative solvers via many HVPs. KFAC replaces these repeated HVPs with a structured curvature estimate, enabling efficient approximate inversion.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We propose a KFAC-based hypergradient method for BO that directly plugs into existing algorithms. It provides rich curvature information at low computational cost—illustrated in <a href="#S1.F1" title="In 1 Introduction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> where KFAC reaches lower test loss with less per-iteration time than iterative IHVP approximations, with only modest memory overhead over HVP-based methods—and is effective on large-scale models across diverse applications. Our main contributions are:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose using inverse KFAC-vector products (IKVPs) for hypergradient computation in IFT-based BO and use them to replace IHVPs in existing methods, yielding a scalable curvature-aware approximation that improves wall-clock efficiency, convergence, and stability under small batch sizes.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We scale data hypercleaning to BERT and show that incorporating curvature improves large-scale performance with only modest time and memory overhead.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We demonstrate broad applicability by applying KFAC (including an empirical variant) to two meta-learning tasks and two ML safety problems, consistently improving performance across domains.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S1.F1.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2603.29108v1/hypergradient_performance_comparison_aistats.svg" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:673/417;" width="673" height="417"></object>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S1.F1.fig2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2603.29108v1/gpu_memory_usage_comparison_aistats.svg" id="S1.F1.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:673/415;" width="673" height="415"></object>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span><span id="S1.F1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Top</span>: KFAC balances accuracy and efficiency
better than Hessian/GGN-based IHVP approximations. <math id="S1.F1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of CG iterations and <math id="S1.F1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> the number of truncated Neumann terms. <span id="S1.F1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Bottom</span>: On BERT, KFAC incurs only a small memory overhead compared to the curvature-free SAMA, while using less memory than CG/Neumann.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Background</h2>

<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Problem Setup.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We formulate BO as a nested problem with an outer variable <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\lambda}\in\mathbb{R}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝝀</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\lambda}\in\mathbb{R}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g., hyperparameters) and an inner variable <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{\theta}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{\theta}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g., model parameters). The goal is to minimize an outer objective <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{J}_{\text{out}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mtext>out</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{J}_{\text{out}}</annotation></semantics></math> that depends on the optimal solution of the inner problem <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{J}_{\text{in}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{J}_{\text{in}}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\min_{\bm{\lambda}\in\mathbb{R}^{m}}\;\Phi(\bm{\lambda})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>𝝀</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.447em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝝀</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\min_{\bm{\lambda}\in\mathbb{R}^{m}}\;\Phi(\bm{\lambda})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle:=\mathcal{J}_{\text{out}}\bigl(\bm{\lambda},{\bm{\theta}}^{\star}(\bm{\lambda})\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mtext>out</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>𝝀</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝝀</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle:=\mathcal{J}_{\text{out}}\bigl(\bm{\lambda},{\bm{\theta}}^{\star}(\bm{\lambda})\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{s.t.}\quad{\bm{\theta}}^{\star}(\bm{\lambda})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝝀</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{s.t.}\quad{\bm{\theta}}^{\star}(\bm{\lambda})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\arg\min_{{\bm{\theta}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}}\mathcal{J}_{\text{in}}(\bm{\lambda},{\bm{\theta}})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></munder></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝝀</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\arg\min_{{\bm{\theta}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}}\mathcal{J}_{\text{in}}(\bm{\lambda},{\bm{\theta}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Here, the outer objective <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{J}_{\text{out}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mtext>out</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{J}_{\text{out}}</annotation></semantics></math> depends on the inner problem’s optimal solution <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{\theta}}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{\theta}}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>. In machine learning, the inner problem typically corresponds to training a neural network <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{f}}({\bm{x}};{\bm{\theta}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒇</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{f}}({\bm{x}};{\bm{\theta}})</annotation></semantics></math> parameterized by <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝜽</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math>, which processes an input <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒙</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{x}}</annotation></semantics></math> into a prediction <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{f}}({\bm{x}},{\bm{\theta}})\in{\mathbb{R}}^{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝒇</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>C</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{f}}({\bm{x}},{\bm{\theta}})\in{\mathbb{R}}^{C}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The prediction is compared to the ground truth <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒚</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{y}}</annotation></semantics></math> with a criterion function <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c:{\bm{f}},{\bm{y}}\mapsto c({\bm{f}},{\bm{y}})\in{\mathbb{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>𝒇</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒇</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c:{\bm{f}},{\bm{y}}\mapsto c({\bm{f}},{\bm{y}})\in{\mathbb{R}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Finally, we form the empirical risk by evaluating, then averaging, the risk over a dataset <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{D}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{D}}</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{L}}_{{\mathcal{D}}}({\bm{\theta}})=\nicefrac{{1}}{{N}}\sum_{n}c({\bm{f}}({\bm{x}}_{n},{\bm{\theta}}),{\bm{y}}_{n})\coloneqq\nicefrac{{1}}{{N}}\sum_{n}\ell_{n}({\bm{\theta}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mpadded voffset="0.3em"><mn mathsize="0.700em">1</mn></mpadded><mpadded style="width:-0.15em;" lspace="-0.1em" width="-0.15em"><mo stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo></mpadded><mi mathsize="0.700em">N</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>n</mi></msub><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝒇</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mrow><mpadded voffset="0.3em"><mn mathsize="0.700em">1</mn></mpadded><mpadded style="width:-0.15em;" lspace="-0.1em" width="-0.15em"><mo stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo></mpadded><mi mathsize="0.700em">N</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>n</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{L}}_{{\mathcal{D}}}({\bm{\theta}})=\nicefrac{{1}}{{N}}\sum_{n}c({\bm{f}}({\bm{x}}_{n},{\bm{\theta}}),{\bm{y}}_{n})\coloneqq\nicefrac{{1}}{{N}}\sum_{n}\ell_{n}({\bm{\theta}})</annotation></semantics></math> with the per-sample loss <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{n}=c_{n}\circ{\bm{f}}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>𝒇</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{n}=c_{n}\circ{\bm{f}}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{n}=c|_{{\bm{y}}={\bm{y}}_{n}},{\bm{f}}_{n}={\bm{f}}|_{{\bm{x}}={\bm{x}}_{n}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒇</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mi>𝒇</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{n}=c|_{{\bm{y}}={\bm{y}}_{n}},{\bm{f}}_{n}={\bm{f}}|_{{\bm{x}}={\bm{x}}_{n}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
One simple example is <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{J}_{\text{in}}(\bm{\lambda},{\bm{\theta}})={\mathcal{L}}_{{\mathcal{D}}}({\bm{\theta}})=\nicefrac{{1}}{{N}}\sum_{n}\ell_{n}({\bm{\theta}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝝀</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mpadded voffset="0.3em"><mn mathsize="0.700em">1</mn></mpadded><mpadded style="width:-0.15em;" lspace="-0.1em" width="-0.15em"><mo stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo></mpadded><mi mathsize="0.700em">N</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>n</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{J}_{\text{in}}(\bm{\lambda},{\bm{\theta}})={\mathcal{L}}_{{\mathcal{D}}}({\bm{\theta}})=\nicefrac{{1}}{{N}}\sum_{n}\ell_{n}({\bm{\theta}})</annotation></semantics></math>, where we may identify <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\lambda}=\{({\bm{x}}_{n},{\bm{y}}_{n})\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝝀</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝒚</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\lambda}=\{({\bm{x}}_{n},{\bm{y}}_{n})\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Hypergradient.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To optimize the outer problem, we require the gradient of <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi(\bm{\lambda})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝝀</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi(\bm{\lambda})</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝝀</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>. By the implicit function theorem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Steven G. Krantz, 2002</a>)</cite>, the hypergradient is:</p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\!\!\nabla\Phi(\bm{\lambda})=\nabla_{1}\mathcal{J}_{\text{out}}(\bm{\lambda},{\bm{\theta}}^{\star}(\bm{\lambda}))-\nabla_{12}^{2}\mathcal{J}_{\text{in}}(\bm{\lambda},{\bm{\theta}}^{\star}(\bm{\lambda})){\bm{v}}^{\star}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝝀</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mtext>out</mtext></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝝀</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝝀</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mn>12</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝝀</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝝀</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝒗</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\!\!\nabla\Phi(\bm{\lambda})=\nabla_{1}\mathcal{J}_{\text{out}}(\bm{\lambda},{\bm{\theta}}^{\star}(\bm{\lambda}))-\nabla_{12}^{2}\mathcal{J}_{\text{in}}(\bm{\lambda},{\bm{\theta}}^{\star}(\bm{\lambda})){\bm{v}}^{\star}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Here <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mo>∇</mo><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the gradient w.r.t the outer variable <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝝀</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mo>∇</mo><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the gradient w.r.t the inner variable <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝜽</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The subscript <sub id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">12</span></sub> indicates mixed second derivatives, while <sub id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_sub"><span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">22</span></sub> denotes the Hessian w.r.t <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝜽</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{v}}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒗</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{v}}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> is the solution of the linear system</p>
<table id="S2.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\nabla_{22}^{2}\mathcal{J}_{\text{in}}(\bm{\lambda},{\bm{\theta}}^{\star}(\bm{\lambda})){\bm{v}}=\nabla_{2}\mathcal{J}_{\text{out}}(\bm{\lambda},{\bm{\theta}}^{\star}(\bm{\lambda}))." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∇</mo><mn>22</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝝀</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝝀</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒗</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mtext>out</mtext></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝝀</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝝀</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\nabla_{22}^{2}\mathcal{J}_{\text{in}}(\bm{\lambda},{\bm{\theta}}^{\star}(\bm{\lambda})){\bm{v}}=\nabla_{2}\mathcal{J}_{\text{out}}(\bm{\lambda},{\bm{\theta}}^{\star}(\bm{\lambda})).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">In practice, <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{v}}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝒗</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{v}}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to an IHVP, which is the main bottleneck in IFT-based bilevel optimization methods. The IFT expression above, <a href="#S2.E2" title="In Hypergradient. ‣ 2 Background" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, holds under standard smoothness and non-degeneracy assumptions: the inner objective must be twice continuously differentiable, and its Hessian at the optimum <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{\theta}}^{\star}(\bm{\lambda})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝝀</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{\theta}}^{\star}(\bm{\lambda})</annotation></semantics></math> must be non-singular, which implies local strong convexity <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Steven G. Krantz, 2002</a>)</cite>. In neural networks, however, the inner optimization is rarely carried to exact optimality because of the cost, so IFT-based hypergradients are usually computed approximately at the current iterate rather than at the true inner solution.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">IHVP approximations.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{H}}\coloneqq\nabla_{22}^{2}\mathcal{J}_{\text{in}}(\bm{\lambda},{\bm{\theta}}^{\star}(\bm{\lambda}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑯</mi><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mn>22</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝝀</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝝀</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{H}}\coloneqq\nabla_{22}^{2}\mathcal{J}_{\text{in}}(\bm{\lambda},{\bm{\theta}}^{\star}(\bm{\lambda}))</annotation></semantics></math> denote the inner problem’s Hessian at <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{\theta}}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{\theta}}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{b}}\coloneqq\nabla_{2}\mathcal{J}_{\text{out}}(\bm{\lambda},{\bm{\theta}}^{\star}(\bm{\lambda}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒃</mi><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mtext>out</mtext></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝝀</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝝀</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{b}}\coloneqq\nabla_{2}\mathcal{J}_{\text{out}}(\bm{\lambda},{\bm{\theta}}^{\star}(\bm{\lambda}))</annotation></semantics></math>.
Computing the hypergradient via <a href="#S2.E1" title="In Hypergradient. ‣ 2 Background" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> requires solving the linear system <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{H}}{\bm{v}}={\bm{b}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝑯</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒗</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝒃</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{H}}{\bm{v}}={\bm{b}}</annotation></semantics></math>, whose exact solution corresponds to the IHVP <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{v}}={\bm{H}}^{-1}{\bm{b}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒗</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝑯</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒃</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{v}}={\bm{H}}^{-1}{\bm{b}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since explicitly forming or inverting <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{H}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{H}}</annotation></semantics></math> is infeasible in high dimensions, iterative methods are commonly used.
They iteratively solve the linear system and require one Hessian-vector product (HVP) per iteration, but their convergence is guaranteed only when <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{H}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{H}}</annotation></semantics></math> is positive semi-definite.
An alternative view is to solve the quadratic problem</p>
<table id="S2.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{v}}^{*}=\argmin_{{\bm{v}}}\frac{1}{2}{\bm{v}}^{\top}{\bm{H}}{\bm{v}}-{\bm{b}}^{\top}{\bm{v}}\,," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝒗</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>min</mi></mrow><mi>𝐯</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐇𝐯</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝐛</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐯</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{v}}^{*}=\argmin_{{\bm{v}}}\frac{1}{2}{\bm{v}}^{\top}{\bm{H}}{\bm{v}}-{\bm{b}}^{\top}{\bm{v}}\,,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">on which iterative solvers such as conjugate gradient (CG) or gradient descent can be applied; this formulation underlies algorithms such as AmIGO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Arbel and Mairal, 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dagréou et al., 2022</a>)</cite>. Other works use the Neumann series</p>
<table id="S2.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{H}}^{-1}=\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}({\bm{I}}-{\bm{H}})^{k}\,," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>𝑯</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></munderover><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝑰</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>𝑯</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{H}}^{-1}=\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}({\bm{I}}-{\bm{H}})^{k}\,,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">and truncate it after <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> terms to obtain an approximate IHVP with <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> HVPs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ji et al., 2021</a>)</cite>, though accuracy deteriorates under ill-conditioning.
Finally, one-step unrolling methods such as DARTS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2019</a>)</cite> and SAMA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Choe et al., 2023b</a>)</cite> approximate <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{H}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{H}}</annotation></semantics></math> by the identity (or equivalently <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=0</annotation></semantics></math>), yielding <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{v}}\approx{\bm{b}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒗</mi><mo>≈</mo><mi>𝒃</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{v}}\approx{\bm{b}}</annotation></semantics></math>, ignoring curvature in exchange for efficiency.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Hessian, GGN, and Fisher.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Consider the inner loss <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{J}}_{\text{in}}(\bm{\lambda},{\bm{\theta}})={\mathcal{L}}_{{\mathcal{D}}}({\bm{\theta}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒥</mi><mtext>in</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝝀</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{J}}_{\text{in}}(\bm{\lambda},{\bm{\theta}})={\mathcal{L}}_{{\mathcal{D}}}({\bm{\theta}})</annotation></semantics></math>.
To calculate the Hessian, applying the chain rule to <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, the empirical risk’s Hessian decomposes into the generalized Gauss-Newton (GGN) matrix <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{G}}({\bm{\theta}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑮</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{G}}({\bm{\theta}})</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schraudolph, 2002</a>)</cite> and a residual term <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{R}}({\bm{\theta}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑹</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{R}}({\bm{\theta}})</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\underbrace{\nabla_{{\bm{\theta}}}^{2}{\mathcal{L}}_{{\mathcal{D}}}({\bm{\theta}})}_{\coloneqq{\bm{H}}({\bm{\theta}})}&amp;=\underbrace{\frac{1}{N}\sum_{n}[{\bm{J}}_{{\bm{\theta}}}{\bm{f}}_{n}]^{\top}(\nabla_{{\bm{f}}}^{2}c_{n})[{\bm{J}}_{{\bm{\theta}}}{\bm{f}}_{n}]}_{\coloneqq{\bm{G}}({\bm{\theta}})}\\
&amp;+\underbrace{\frac{1}{N}\sum_{n}\sum_{i=1}^{C}[\nabla_{{\bm{f}}}c_{n}]_{i}\nabla_{{\bm{\theta}}}^{2}[{\bm{f}}_{n}({\bm{\theta}})]_{i}}_{\coloneqq{\bm{R}}({\bm{\theta}})}\,.\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><msubsup><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mi>𝑯</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mi>n</mi></munder><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑱</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝒇</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>𝒇</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑱</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝒇</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mi>𝑮</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mi>n</mi></munder><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>C</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>𝒇</mi></msub><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒇</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mi>𝑹</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\underbrace{\nabla_{{\bm{\theta}}}^{2}{\mathcal{L}}_{{\mathcal{D}}}({\bm{\theta}})}_{\coloneqq{\bm{H}}({\bm{\theta}})}&amp;=\underbrace{\frac{1}{N}\sum_{n}[{\bm{J}}_{{\bm{\theta}}}{\bm{f}}_{n}]^{\top}(\nabla_{{\bm{f}}}^{2}c_{n})[{\bm{J}}_{{\bm{\theta}}}{\bm{f}}_{n}]}_{\coloneqq{\bm{G}}({\bm{\theta}})}\\
&amp;+\underbrace{\frac{1}{N}\sum_{n}\sum_{i=1}^{C}[\nabla_{{\bm{f}}}c_{n}]_{i}\nabla_{{\bm{\theta}}}^{2}[{\bm{f}}_{n}({\bm{\theta}})]_{i}}_{\coloneqq{\bm{R}}({\bm{\theta}})}\,.\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{J}}_{{\bm{\theta}}}{\bm{f}}_{n}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{C\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝑱</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝒇</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{J}}_{{\bm{\theta}}}{\bm{f}}_{n}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{C\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> is the Jacobian containing the partial derivatives of <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{f}}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒇</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{f}}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> w.r.t. <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{\theta}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝜽</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{\theta}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The GGN is often used instead of the Hessian as it is positive semi-definite. Moreover, for common losses such as mean-squared error and softmax cross-entropy, the GGN coincides with the Fisher information matrix <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">Martens, 2020</a>, FIM,)</cite></p>
<table id="S2.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center">
<div id="S2.Ex6.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_markedasmath ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:218.9pt;height:12pt;vertical-align:-3.4pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-5.8pt,0.3pt) scale(0.9,0.9) ;">
<p id="S2.Ex6.1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.Ex6.m1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\!{\bm{F}}({\bm{\theta}})\!=\!\mathbb{E}_{{\bm{x}}\sim p_{\text{data}},{\bm{y}}\sim p_{\bm{\theta}}(\cdot|{\bm{x}})}\big[\nabla_{{\bm{\theta}}}\log p_{\bm{\theta}}({\bm{y}}|{\bm{x}})\,\nabla_{{\bm{\theta}}}\log p_{\bm{\theta}}({\bm{y}}|{\bm{x}})^{\top}\big]\!" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑭</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">=</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>𝒙</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>data</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><mi>log</mi><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>𝜽</mi></msub><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\!{\bm{F}}({\bm{\theta}})\!=\!\mathbb{E}_{{\bm{x}}\sim p_{\text{data}},{\bm{y}}\sim p_{\bm{\theta}}(\cdot|{\bm{x}})}\big[\nabla_{{\bm{\theta}}}\log p_{\bm{\theta}}({\bm{y}}|{\bm{x}})\,\nabla_{{\bm{\theta}}}\log p_{\bm{\theta}}({\bm{y}}|{\bm{x}})^{\top}\big]\!</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</span></div></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\text{data}}({\bm{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>data</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\text{data}}({\bm{x}})</annotation></semantics></math> is the input distribution and <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\theta}({\bm{y}}|{\bm{x}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝒚</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>𝒙</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\theta}({\bm{y}}|{\bm{x}})</annotation></semantics></math> is the model’s predictive distribution. The Fisher view underlies natural gradient descent <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Amari, 1998</a>)</cite> and motivates KFAC as a preconditioner for neural network optimization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">Martens and Grosse, 2015</a>)</cite>. While both the Hessian and GGN are prohibitively expensive to store, automatic differentiation allows cheap evaluation of matrix-vector products at the same complexity as computing the gradient <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pearlmutter, 1994</a>; <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schraudolph, 2002</a>; <a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dagréou et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. However, this computation is still expensive for BO as it requires applying an inverse at every outer iteration.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Method</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Hypergradient computation in BO requires IHVPs, which are expensive or unstable with iterative solvers.
We replace them with IKVPs, enabled by a layer-wise Kronecker-factored approximation of the GGN/Fisher.
<a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 KFAC: a Layer-wise Curvature Approximation ‣ 3 Method" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> recalls KFAC, <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Replacing IHVP with IKVP ‣ 3 Method" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> shows how to integrate IKVPs into BO, and <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 Diagnostic Study of IHVP Approximations ‣ 3 Method" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> provides a diagnostic study that motivates and illustrates its robustness under ill-conditioning.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>KFAC: a Layer-wise Curvature Approximation</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The Hessian <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{H}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{H}}</annotation></semantics></math> or GGN <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{G}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{G}}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\times d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\times d</annotation></semantics></math>, which is prohibitively large for modern architectures.
KFAC reduces this cost by adopting a layer-wise approximation, treating each layer independently and ignoring cross-layer interactions.
This yields a block-diagonal structure, where each block is further approximated with a Kronecker product.
This structure is particularly attractive for BO, since hypergradients require repeated curvature inverses: KFAC replaces expensive iterative solvers with direct structured inverses, enabling curvature information at low cost.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">KFAC for a single fully-connected layer.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Here we consider the case for a fully-connected layer inside a neural net. The layer’s weights are <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{W}}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{d_{1}\times d_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{W}}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{d_{1}\times d_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> (we omit biases for simplicity) and it processes an input <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{a}}_{n}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{d_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒂</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{a}}_{n}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{d_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> into an output <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{z}}_{n}={\bm{W}}{\bm{a}}_{n}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{d_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝑾</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝒂</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{z}}_{n}={\bm{W}}{\bm{a}}_{n}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{d_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Further, let <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\operatorname{vec}({\bm{W}})\in{\mathbb{R}}^{d_{1}d_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>vec</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑾</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\operatorname{vec}({\bm{W}})\in{\mathbb{R}}^{d_{1}d_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the flattened weights from stacking the rows into a vector.
We can then write down the GGN matrix <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{G}}(\operatorname{vec}{\bm{W}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑮</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>vec</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>𝑾</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{G}}(\operatorname{vec}{\bm{W}})</annotation></semantics></math> w.r.t. to the layer’s parameters analytically.
To do so, we use the chain rule (<math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{J}}_{\operatorname{vec}{\bm{W}}}{\bm{f}}_{n}=({\bm{J}}_{{\bm{z}}_{n}}{\bm{f}}_{n})({\bm{J}}_{\operatorname{vec}{\bm{W}}}{\bm{z}}_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝑱</mi><mrow><mi>vec</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>𝑾</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝒇</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑱</mi><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝒇</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑱</mi><mrow><mi>vec</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>𝑾</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{J}}_{\operatorname{vec}{\bm{W}}}{\bm{f}}_{n}=({\bm{J}}_{{\bm{z}}_{n}}{\bm{f}}_{n})({\bm{J}}_{\operatorname{vec}{\bm{W}}}{\bm{z}}_{n})</annotation></semantics></math>) with analytical output-parameter Jacobian <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{J}}_{\operatorname{vec}{\bm{W}}}{\bm{z}}_{n}={\bm{I}}_{d_{1}}\otimes{\bm{a}}_{n}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝑱</mi><mrow><mi>vec</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>𝑾</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑰</mi><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒂</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{J}}_{\operatorname{vec}{\bm{W}}}{\bm{z}}_{n}={\bm{I}}_{d_{1}}\otimes{\bm{a}}_{n}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dangel et al., 2020a</a>)</cite>, yielding that each datum contributes a single Kronecker product:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\bm{G}}(\operatorname{vec}{\bm{W}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑮</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>vec</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>𝑾</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\bm{G}}(\operatorname{vec}{\bm{W}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{n}({\bm{J}}_{{\bm{z}}_{n}}{\bm{f}}_{n})^{\top}(\nabla_{{\bm{f}}}^{2}c_{n})({\bm{J}}_{{\bm{z}}_{n}}{\bm{f}}_{n})\otimes{\bm{a}}_{n}{\bm{a}}_{n}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>n</mi></munder></mstyle><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑱</mi><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝒇</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>𝒇</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑱</mi><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝒇</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>𝒂</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒂</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{n}({\bm{J}}_{{\bm{z}}_{n}}{\bm{f}}_{n})^{\top}(\nabla_{{\bm{f}}}^{2}c_{n})({\bm{J}}_{{\bm{z}}_{n}}{\bm{f}}_{n})\otimes{\bm{a}}_{n}{\bm{a}}_{n}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\hat{\mathbb{E}}\!\left[({\bm{J}}_{{\bm{z}}}{\bm{f}})^{\top}(\nabla_{{\bm{f}}}^{2}c)({\bm{J}}_{{\bm{z}}}{\bm{f}})\otimes{\bm{a}}{\bm{a}}^{\top}\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑱</mi><mi>𝒛</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒇</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>𝒇</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mi>c</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑱</mi><mi>𝒛</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒇</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>𝒂</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝒂</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\hat{\mathbb{E}}\!\left[({\bm{J}}_{{\bm{z}}}{\bm{f}})^{\top}(\nabla_{{\bm{f}}}^{2}c)({\bm{J}}_{{\bm{z}}}{\bm{f}})\otimes{\bm{a}}{\bm{a}}^{\top}\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where we use the notation <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{E}}[\bullet]=\nicefrac{{1}}{{N}}\sum_{n}\bullet_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∙</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mpadded voffset="0.3em"><mn mathsize="0.700em">1</mn></mpadded><mpadded style="width:-0.15em;" lspace="-0.1em" width="-0.15em"><mo stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo></mpadded><mi mathsize="0.700em">N</mi></mrow><msub><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mo lspace="0em">∙</mo><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{E}}[\bullet]=\nicefrac{{1}}{{N}}\sum_{n}\bullet_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> to indicate the expectation over the dataset.
To obtain a single Kronecker product, KFAC makes the expectation approximation <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\hat{\mathbb{E}}[\bullet\otimes\star]\approx\hat{\mathbb{E}}[\bullet]\otimes\hat{\mathbb{E}}[\star]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∙</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⊗</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋆</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∙</mo><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋆</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbb{E}}[\bullet\otimes\star]\approx\hat{\mathbb{E}}[\bullet]\otimes\hat{\mathbb{E}}[\star]</annotation></semantics></math>.
In summary, this yields the following approximation of the GGN:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\bm{G}}(\operatorname{vec}{\bm{W}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑮</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>vec</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>𝑾</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\bm{G}}(\operatorname{vec}{\bm{W}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\approx{\bm{B}}_{\text{KFAC}}\otimes{\bm{A}}_{\text{KFAC}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑩</mi><mtext>KFAC</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>𝑨</mi><mtext>KFAC</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\approx{\bm{B}}_{\text{KFAC}}\otimes{\bm{A}}_{\text{KFAC}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody><tr class="ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline"><td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_left" style="white-space:normal;" colspan="5">where</td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\bm{B}}_{\text{KFAC}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑩</mi><mtext>KFAC</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\bm{B}}_{\text{KFAC}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{n}({\bm{J}}_{{\bm{z}}_{n}}{\bm{f}}_{n})^{\top}(\nabla_{{\bm{f}}}^{2}c_{n})({\bm{J}}_{{\bm{z}}_{n}}{\bm{f}}_{n})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>n</mi></munder></mstyle><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑱</mi><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝒇</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>𝒇</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑱</mi><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝒇</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{n}({\bm{J}}_{{\bm{z}}_{n}}{\bm{f}}_{n})^{\top}(\nabla_{{\bm{f}}}^{2}c_{n})({\bm{J}}_{{\bm{z}}_{n}}{\bm{f}}_{n}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\bm{A}}_{\text{KFAC}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑨</mi><mtext>KFAC</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\bm{A}}_{\text{KFAC}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{n}{\bm{a}}_{n}{\bm{a}}_{n}^{\top}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>n</mi></munder></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>𝒂</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒂</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{n}{\bm{a}}_{n}{\bm{a}}_{n}^{\top}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">This structural approximation reduces the storage cost of <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{G}}(\operatorname{vec}{\bm{W}})\in{\mathbb{R}}^{d_{1}d_{2}\times d_{1}d_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝑮</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>vec</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>𝑾</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{G}}(\operatorname{vec}{\bm{W}})\in{\mathbb{R}}^{d_{1}d_{2}\times d_{1}d_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> to the much smaller Kronecker factors <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{A}}_{\text{KFAC}}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{d_{2}\times d_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑨</mi><mtext>KFAC</mtext></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{A}}_{\text{KFAC}}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{d_{2}\times d_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{B}}_{\text{KFAC}}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{d_{1}\times d_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑩</mi><mtext>KFAC</mtext></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{B}}_{\text{KFAC}}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{d_{1}\times d_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math> for each layer. Inverting KFAC resorts to inverting the two Kronecker factors, since <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="({\bm{B}}\otimes{\bm{A}})^{-1}={\bm{B}}^{-1}\otimes{\bm{A}}^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝑩</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mi>𝑨</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝑩</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msup><mi>𝑨</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">({\bm{B}}\otimes{\bm{A}})^{-1}={\bm{B}}^{-1}\otimes{\bm{A}}^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. For a network with multiple layers, KFAC applies this construction layerwise and combines the resulting factors into a block-diagonal approximation of the full network’s curvature.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Computational details.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The (uncentered) covariance of the layer inputs, <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{A}}_{\text{KFAC}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑨</mi><mtext>KFAC</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{A}}_{\text{KFAC}}</annotation></semantics></math>, is easy to compute in a forward pass.
For the first factor <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{B}}_{\text{KFAC}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑩</mi><mtext>KFAC</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{B}}_{\text{KFAC}}</annotation></semantics></math>, there exist multiple variants that differ in computational cost (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dangel et al. 2025b</a></cite> for a detailed introduction).
The most expensive one <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Botev et al., 2017</a>)</cite> employs a symmetric factorization of the criterion’s Hessian into <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\smash{\nabla_{{\bm{f}}}^{2}}c_{n}=\nicefrac{{1}}{{C}}\smash{\sum_{i=1}^{C}}{\bm{s}}_{n,i}{\bm{s}}_{n,i}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝒇</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mpadded voffset="0.3em"><mn mathsize="0.700em">1</mn></mpadded><mpadded style="width:-0.15em;" lspace="-0.1em" width="-0.15em"><mo stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo></mpadded><mi mathsize="0.700em">C</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>C</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\smash{\nabla_{{\bm{f}}}^{2}}c_{n}=\nicefrac{{1}}{{C}}\smash{\sum_{i=1}^{C}}{\bm{s}}_{n,i}{\bm{s}}_{n,i}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>, then computes the covariance of the pseudo-gradients <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\smash{\tilde{{\bm{g}}}_{n,i}\coloneqq({\bm{J}}_{{\bm{z}}_{n}}{\bm{f}}_{n})^{\top}{\bm{s}}_{n,i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒈</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≔</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑱</mi><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝒇</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\smash{\tilde{{\bm{g}}}_{n,i}\coloneqq({\bm{J}}_{{\bm{z}}_{n}}{\bm{f}}_{n})^{\top}{\bm{s}}_{n,i}}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e. <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{B}}_{\text{KFAC}}=\nicefrac{{1}}{{NC}}\sum_{n}\sum_{i}\tilde{{\bm{g}}}_{n,i}\tilde{{\bm{g}}}_{n,i}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑩</mi><mtext>KFAC</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mpadded voffset="0.3em"><mn mathsize="0.700em">1</mn></mpadded><mpadded style="width:-0.15em;" lspace="-0.1em" width="-0.15em"><mo stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo></mpadded><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">C</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mi>n</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒈</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝒈</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{B}}_{\text{KFAC}}=\nicefrac{{1}}{{NC}}\sum_{n}\sum_{i}\tilde{{\bm{g}}}_{n,i}\tilde{{\bm{g}}}_{n,i}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>.
This incurs <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> backpropagations per datum.
We will follow the original approach of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">Martens and Grosse (2015)</a></cite>, which introduces a randomization via <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p({\bm{s}}_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p({\bm{s}}_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\smash{\nabla_{{\bm{f}}}^{2}c_{n}=\mathbb{E}_{{\bm{s}}_{n,i}\sim p({\bm{s}}_{n})}[{\bm{s}}_{n,i}{\bm{s}}_{n,i}^{\top}]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝒇</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\smash{\nabla_{{\bm{f}}}^{2}c_{n}=\mathbb{E}_{{\bm{s}}_{n,i}\sim p({\bm{s}}_{n})}[{\bm{s}}_{n,i}{\bm{s}}_{n,i}^{\top}]}</annotation></semantics></math>, then approximates the expectation with a Monte-Carlo estimate involving <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> samples, i.e. <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{{\bm{f}}}^{2}c_{n}\approx\nicefrac{{1}}{{M}}\sum_{i}{\bm{s}}_{n,i}{\bm{s}}_{n,i}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝒇</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mrow><mrow><mpadded voffset="0.3em"><mn mathsize="0.700em">1</mn></mpadded><mpadded style="width:-0.15em;" lspace="-0.1em" width="-0.15em"><mo stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo></mpadded><mi mathsize="0.700em">M</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒔</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{{\bm{f}}}^{2}c_{n}\approx\nicefrac{{1}}{{M}}\sum_{i}{\bm{s}}_{n,i}{\bm{s}}_{n,i}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{s}}_{n,i}\smash{\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\text{i.i.d.{} }}}{{\sim}}}p({\bm{s}}_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mover><mo>∼</mo><mtext>i.i.d. </mtext></mover><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{s}}_{n,i}\smash{\stackrel{{\scriptstyle\text{i.i.d.{} }}}{{\sim}}}p({\bm{s}}_{n})</annotation></semantics></math>.
This reduces the number of backpropagations from <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, and we will use <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=1</annotation></semantics></math> as is common practice.
The distribution <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p({\bm{s}}_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p({\bm{s}}_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> is determined by the probabilistic interpretation of the loss function (e.g., Gaussian for MSE; the explicit forms are given in <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Probabilistic Interpretation of Losses and Monte-Carlo KFAC" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We also adopt the empirical variant of KFAC (KFAC EMP), which constructs <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{B}}_{\text{KFAC}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑩</mi><mtext>KFAC</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{B}}_{\text{KFAC}}</annotation></semantics></math> from empirical, rather than sampled, gradients. Its main computational advantage is that it reuses the standard backward pass already needed for gradient computation, while Monte-Carlo GGN variants typically require an additional backward pass with sampled curvature probes. The empirical <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{B}}_{\text{KFAC}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑩</mi><mtext>KFAC</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{B}}_{\text{KFAC}}</annotation></semantics></math> is estimated as</p>
<table id="S3.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{B}}_{\text{KFAC}}=\tfrac{1}{N}\sum\nolimits_{n}{\bm{g}}_{n}{\bm{g}}_{n}^{\top},\qquad{\bm{g}}_{n}=\nabla_{{\bm{z}}_{n}}\ell_{n}({\bm{\theta}})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝑩</mi><mtext>KFAC</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>n</mi></msub><mrow><msub><mi>𝒈</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒈</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝒈</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><msub><mi>𝒛</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></msub><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝜽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{B}}_{\text{KFAC}}=\tfrac{1}{N}\sum\nolimits_{n}{\bm{g}}_{n}{\bm{g}}_{n}^{\top},\qquad{\bm{g}}_{n}=\nabla_{{\bm{z}}_{n}}\ell_{n}({\bm{\theta}}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">with <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{g}}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒈</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{g}}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> denoting the per-example gradient of the loss with respect to the layer pre-activations.
The remaining components of KFAC, namely the Kronecker factorization, damping, and matrix inversion, are unchanged.
Empirical Fisher approximations of this form are widely used in practical second-order optimization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">George et al., 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2022</a>)</cite>.
We compare KFAC EMP with standard KFAC in <a href="#S5" title="5 Curvature Matters at Scale" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, and use KFAC EMP in all experiments in <a href="#S6" title="6 Broader Applicability" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> due to the size of problems.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">KFAC approximation accuracy.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For linear networks with square loss, the Hessian, GGN, and KFAC are identical <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Petersen et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, yielding exact natural-gradient updates and IHVPs. This exactness further extends to deep linear networks: despite nonlinearity in parameters, KFAC recovers the exact natural gradient up to a constant factor, and is equivalent to the block-diagonal GGN in this setting <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bernacchia et al., 2018</a>)</cite>. Beyond these regimes, KFAC provides a structured approximation to the GGN for deep nonlinear networks, preserving curvature information at layerwise granularity while remaining computattionally tractable. Moreover, in the infinite-width in the NTK regime <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jacot et al., 2018</a>)</cite>, approximate natural gradient descent via KFAC achieves the same convergence rate as exact gradient methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Karakida and Osawa, 2020</a>)</cite>. Our work focuses on practical large-scale regimes where exact solvers are infeasible, and evaluates how effectively this approximation supports hypergradient computation in BO.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Replacing IHVP with IKVP</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We focus on BO problems where hypergradients can be expressed, under the implicit function theorem, as in equation <a href="#S2.E1" title="Equation 1 ‣ Hypergradient. ‣ 2 Background" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. Instead of approximating IHVP <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{H}}^{-1}{\bm{b}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝑯</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒃</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{H}}^{-1}{\bm{b}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we follow the common practice of approximating <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{H}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑯</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{H}}</annotation></semantics></math> with the GGN matrix <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{G}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{G}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and approximating
<math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{G}}^{-1}{\bm{b}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝑮</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒃</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{G}}^{-1}{\bm{b}}</annotation></semantics></math> instead.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To scale this computation, we further approximate <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{G}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{G}}</annotation></semantics></math> with a Kronecker-factored block-diagonal estimate <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{{\bm{G}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑮</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{{\bm{G}}}</annotation></semantics></math> obtained via KFAC. Suppose we would like to apply <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{{\bm{G}}}^{-1}{\bm{v}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑮</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒗</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{{\bm{G}}}^{-1}{\bm{v}}</annotation></semantics></math> for some parameter vector <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{v}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝒗</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{v}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{{\bm{G}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑮</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{{\bm{G}}}</annotation></semantics></math> is block diagonal, for simplicity, we can just consider a single layer case: for <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{W}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{1}\times d_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑾</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{W}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{1}\times d_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{A}}_{\text{KFAC}}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{d_{2}\times d_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑨</mi><mtext>KFAC</mtext></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{A}}_{\text{KFAC}}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{d_{2}\times d_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the uncentered covariance matrix of the activation and <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{B}}_{\text{KFAC}}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{d_{1}\times d_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝑩</mi><mtext>KFAC</mtext></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{B}}_{\text{KFAC}}\in{\mathbb{R}}^{d_{1}\times d_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the gradient covariances. By construction, the KFAC block takes the form <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{{\bm{G}}}={\bm{B}}_{\text{KFAC}}\otimes{\bm{A}}_{\text{KFAC}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑮</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑩</mi><mtext>KFAC</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi>𝑨</mi><mtext>KFAC</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{{\bm{G}}}={\bm{B}}_{\text{KFAC}}\otimes{\bm{A}}_{\text{KFAC}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Given a reshaped vector <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{v}}=\operatorname{vec}({\bm{V}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒗</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>vec</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝑽</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{v}}=\operatorname{vec}({\bm{V}})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{V}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝑽</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{V}}</annotation></semantics></math> matches the shape of the layer’s weights, the IKVP follows from Kronecker identities as</p>
<table id="S3.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{{\bm{G}}}^{-1}{\bm{v}}=({\bm{B}}_{\text{KFAC}}^{-1}\otimes{\bm{A}}_{\text{KFAC}}^{-1}){\bm{v}}=\operatorname{vec}\big({\bm{B}}_{\text{KFAC}}^{-1}{\bm{V}}{\bm{A}}_{\text{KFAC}}^{-1}\big)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑮</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒗</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝑩</mi><mtext>KFAC</mtext><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msubsup><mi>𝑨</mi><mtext>KFAC</mtext><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝒗</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>vec</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝑩</mi><mtext>KFAC</mtext><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑽</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝑨</mi><mtext>KFAC</mtext><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{{\bm{G}}}^{-1}{\bm{v}}=({\bm{B}}_{\text{KFAC}}^{-1}\otimes{\bm{A}}_{\text{KFAC}}^{-1}){\bm{v}}=\operatorname{vec}\big({\bm{B}}_{\text{KFAC}}^{-1}{\bm{V}}{\bm{A}}_{\text{KFAC}}^{-1}\big).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Thus, instead of applying the inverse of a full <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{1}d_{2}\times d_{1}d_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{1}d_{2}\times d_{1}d_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> matrix, IKVP reduces to two smaller inversions and a sequence of matrix multiplications.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2603.29108v1/four_panel_comparison_resnet.svg" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:673/169;" width="673" height="169"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Results on data hypercleaning. <span id="S3.F2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Left two panels:</span> existing BO algorithms improve when their IHVP solvers are replaced with IKVPs. <span id="S3.F2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Right two panels:</span> KFAC-based methods compared against other non-IFT baselines, showing faster convergence in both time and iterations. Curves are truncated at the lowest test loss reached. Solid black line is trained on the validation dataset, while dashed blackline is trained on the validation dataset and the noisy dataset together.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Computational Cost of IKVP.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For each layer, inverting <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{A}}_{\text{KFAC}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑨</mi><mtext>KFAC</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{A}}_{\text{KFAC}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{B}}_{\text{KFAC}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑩</mi><mtext>KFAC</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{B}}_{\text{KFAC}}</annotation></semantics></math> requires <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(d_{1}^{3}+d_{2}^{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(d_{1}^{3}+d_{2}^{3})</annotation></semantics></math> operations. Once inverted, applying an IKVP to a vector costs <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(d_{1}^{2}d_{2}+d_{1}d_{2}^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(d_{1}^{2}d_{2}+d_{1}d_{2}^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>, corresponding to two matrix-matrix multiplications. Performing forward and backward passes is <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(Bd_{1}d_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(Bd_{1}d_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> is the batch size, so this operation has similar complexity to backpropagation when <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are similar to <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Damping.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Curvature approximations are known to be singular for neural nets <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sagun et al., 2016</a>)</cite>. To stabilize inversion, we use the factored Tikhonov damping, which adds scaled identity to each Kronecker factor, rather than to the full block, which preserves efficiency while improving conditioning. In our implementation, we adopt the <em id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">heuristic damping</em> strategy from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">Martens and Grosse (2015)</a></cite>, where the relative scaling between the activation covariance <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{A}}_{\text{KFAC}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑨</mi><mtext>KFAC</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{A}}_{\text{KFAC}}</annotation></semantics></math> and the gradient covariance <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{B}}_{\text{KFAC}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑩</mi><mtext>KFAC</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{B}}_{\text{KFAC}}</annotation></semantics></math> is controlled by a parameter <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> derived from their traces. Concretely, the damping applied to <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{A}}_{\text{KFAC}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑨</mi><mtext>KFAC</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{A}}_{\text{KFAC}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{B}}_{\text{KFAC}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝑩</mi><mtext>KFAC</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{B}}_{\text{KFAC}}</annotation></semantics></math> is</p>
<table id="S3.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{A}}_{\text{KFAC}}\rightarrow{\bm{A}}_{\text{KFAC}}+\pi\sqrt{\lambda}{\bm{I}},\quad{\bm{B}}_{\text{KFAC}}\rightarrow{\bm{B}}_{\text{KFAC}}+\tfrac{1}{\pi}\sqrt{\lambda}{\bm{I}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝑨</mi><mtext>KFAC</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑨</mi><mtext>KFAC</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mi>λ</mi></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑰</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑩</mi><mtext>KFAC</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝑩</mi><mtext>KFAC</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>π</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mi>λ</mi></msqrt><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑰</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{A}}_{\text{KFAC}}\rightarrow{\bm{A}}_{\text{KFAC}}+\pi\sqrt{\lambda}{\bm{I}},\quad{\bm{B}}_{\text{KFAC}}\rightarrow{\bm{B}}_{\text{KFAC}}+\tfrac{1}{\pi}\sqrt{\lambda}{\bm{I}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">with <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi=\sqrt{\nicefrac{{d_{2}\text{Tr}({\bm{A}}_{\text{KFAC}})}}{{d_{1}\text{Tr}({\bm{B}}_{\text{KFAC}})}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mpadded voffset="0.3em"><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">d</mi><mn mathsize="0.710em">2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext mathsize="0.700em">Tr</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">𝑨</mi><mtext mathsize="0.710em">KFAC</mtext></msub><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mpadded><mpadded style="width:-0.15em;" lspace="-0.1em" width="-0.15em"><mo stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo></mpadded><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">d</mi><mn mathsize="0.710em">1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext mathsize="0.700em">Tr</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">𝑩</mi><mtext mathsize="0.710em">KFAC</mtext></msub><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi=\sqrt{\nicefrac{{d_{2}\text{Tr}({\bm{A}}_{\text{KFAC}})}}{{d_{1}\text{Tr}({\bm{B}}_{\text{KFAC}})}}}</annotation></semantics></math> balancing the two factors. Heuristic damping has been shown to perform more robustly than naive Tikhonov damping <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">Martens and Grosse, 2015</a>)</cite>, while adding negligible computational overhead.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">there exist many public source implementations of KFAC <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dangel et al., 2020b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">Osawa et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Grosse et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib66" title="" class="ltx_ref">Botev and Martens, 2022</a>)</cite>.
We use the <span id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">curvlinops</span> package <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dangel et al., 2025a</a>)</cite>, which provides easy access to VPs products with KFAC and its damped inverse.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Diagnostic Study of IHVP Approximations</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A key practical advantage of KFAC arises from its robustness to ill-conditioning in the inner-level curvature.
To isolate this effect, we consider a controlled setting where the exact inverse Hessian is available and
compare different IHVP approximations under increasing dimensionality.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We study linear regression with square loss, where both design matrix <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{X}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝑿</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{X}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times N}</annotation></semantics></math> and targets <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{y}}\in\mathbb{R}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒚</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>N</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{y}}\in\mathbb{R}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> are generated with i.i.d. standard Gaussian entries. We fit <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{w}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒘</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{w}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> using the square loss. The Hessian is known in closed form and only depends on the data matrix:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{H}}=\frac{1}{N}{\bm{X}}{\bm{X}}^{\top}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝑯</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑿</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝑿</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{H}}=\frac{1}{N}{\bm{X}}{\bm{X}}^{\top}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">We vary the parameter dimension <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> while keeping the dataset size <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> to 100. In this setting, KFAC is an exact approximation of the Hessian <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Petersen et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. As <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> grows relative to <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>, the Hessian becomes increasingly ill-conditioned and eventually rank-deficient, a regime commonly encountered in over-parameterized models.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We compare Monte-Carlo KFAC against truncated Neumann series
and CG under fixed computational budgets.
Accuracy is measured by the relative operator-norm error</p>
<table id="S3.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min_{\alpha}\|\alpha(\widehat{{\bm{H}}}+\lambda{\bm{I}})^{-1}-({\bm{H}}+\lambda{\bm{I}})^{-1}\|_{2}/\|({\bm{H}}+\lambda{\bm{I}})^{-1}\|_{2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mi>α</mi></munder><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝑯</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑰</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝑯</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑰</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>/</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝑯</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝑰</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min_{\alpha}\|\alpha(\widehat{{\bm{H}}}+\lambda{\bm{I}})^{-1}-({\bm{H}}+\lambda{\bm{I}})^{-1}\|_{2}/\|({\bm{H}}+\lambda{\bm{I}})^{-1}\|_{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> is searched to account for the step-size in training and <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=10^{-5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=10^{-5}</annotation></semantics></math> is the damping factor.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S3.T1" title="In 3.3 Diagnostic Study of IHVP Approximations ‣ 3 Method" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> highlights two consistent trends. First, while CG and Neumann solvers can achieve high accuracy at small <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> given sufficient iterations,
their error degrades rapidly as <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> increases under fixed iteration budgets.
This behavior is expected, as the convergence rate of iterative solvers depends on the Hessian’s condition number <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bakushinsky and Kokurin, 2004</a>)</cite>.
Second, KFAC maintains stable accuracy as <math id="S3.SS3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> increases, achieving orders-of-magnitude lower error
than budget-limited CG and Neumann solvers.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Relative error for different IHVP approximations as the parameter dimension <math id="S3.T1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> increases. Neu-<math id="S3.T1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> denotes a truncated Neumann series with <math id="S3.T1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> terms. CG-<math id="S3.T1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> denotes conjugate gradient with <math id="S3.T1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> iterations. Identity corresponds to the identity approximation used in one-step unrolling.</figcaption>
<table id="S3.T1.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S3.T1.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;">Method</th>
<td id="S3.T1.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><math id="S3.T1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=10</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><math id="S3.T1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=100</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><math id="S3.T1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=500" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>500</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=500</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;">KFAC</th>
<td id="S3.T1.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><math id="S3.T1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7.62\text{\times}{10}^{-3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>7.62</mn><mtext>×</mtext><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7.62\text{\times}{10}^{-3}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><math id="S3.T1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7.62\text{\times}{10}^{-3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>7.62</mn><mtext>×</mtext><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7.62\text{\times}{10}^{-3}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><math id="S3.T1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7.62\text{\times}{10}^{-3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>7.62</mn><mtext>×</mtext><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7.62\text{\times}{10}^{-3}</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;">Neu-3</th>
<td id="S3.T1.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><math id="S3.T1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.12\text{\times}{10}^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.12</mn><mtext>×</mtext><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.12\text{\times}{10}^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><math id="S3.T1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2.45\text{\times}{10}^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.45</mn><mtext>×</mtext><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.45\text{\times}{10}^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><math id="S3.T1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8.01\text{\times}{10}^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>8.01</mn><mtext>×</mtext><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8.01\text{\times}{10}^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;">Neu-20</th>
<td id="S3.T1.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left" style="padding-left:4.0pt;padding-right:4.0pt;"><math id="S3.T1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2.22\text{\times}{10}^{-2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.22</mn><mtext>×</mtext><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.22\text{\times}{10}^{-2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
